<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Qi (Five Dynasties)</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 924 CE · capital Fengxiang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the warlord Li Maozhen west of Chang'an, which stayed loyal to the memory of the Tang and refused to recognise the Later Liang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a>, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Wentong, rose as an officer in the imperial guard and became military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengxiang">Fengxiang</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in 887, receiving the imperial surname Li. He was made Prince of Qi in 901 and that year took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhaozong-of-tang">Emperor Zhaozong</a> into his custody until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen's</a> siege of Fengxiang forced him to hand him over in 903.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> fell in 907, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a> kept using the Tang reign era and the title Prince of Qi but never declared himself emperor. He ran a princely court with appointed officials. His domain shrank steadily as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-shu">Former Shu</a> took his southern prefectures, and he relied on alliances with the state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a> submitted in 924 and was confirmed as Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. He died later that year, and his son Li Congyan succeeded him only as an ordinary provincial governor.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qi-five-dynasties.jpg" alt="Qi (Five Dynasties)" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fengxiang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Maozhen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qi (Li Maozhen)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=916&amp;focus=qi-five-dynasties" aria-label="Qi (Five Dynasties) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7409 928 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qi (Five Dynasties) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7729 1184L7898 1125"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7898 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7729 1184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7729 1184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=916&amp;focus=qi-five-dynasties">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
